<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Helvetii</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Helvetians, Elouetioi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Helvetii were a Celtic tribe or tribal confederation that occupied most of the Swiss Plateau when they came into contact with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> in the 1st century BCE. They had no kings but seem to have been ruled by a class of noblemen. In 58 BCE they burned their homes and set out to settle in southwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, a migration that served as a catalyst for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>'s conquest of Gaul; defeated near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bibracte">Bibracte</a>, they were sent back to rebuild their homes, and Caesar's own figures, which modern scholars think too high, claim only about 30 percent survived. Subjugated after 52 BCE and largely Romanised by the 2nd century, they were later assimilated by the Alemanni and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Lepontic in northern Italy used an alphabet adapted from the Etruscan, Celtiberian was written in the Iberian script, and in southern Gaul Gaulish was written in Greek letters; Caesar found lists of the <b>Helvetii</b> written in Greek letters.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>Each tribe had a council of elders and at first a king; by Caesar’s day several, such as the Aedui and the <b>Helvetii</b>, were governed instead by magistrates elected every year.</blockquote><a href="/societies/gaul">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Gaul</a></li></ul></section>
